preConditionHook:
// BROOKS'S SPIKE WITH LITTLE OVERLAP: every one of the last spikeBars bars is
// a bull trend bar whose body is at least bodyFraction of its own range, AND
// each bar of the run holds its low at or within maxOverlapTicks ticks of the
// PREVIOUS bar's low. The oldest bar of the run has no predecessor inside the
// run, so the overlap test is skipped there -- that is what the kk < spikeBars
// - 1 guard says.
//
// THE PAIRWISE TERM IS WHY THIS IS NOT ConsecutiveStrongTrendBars ANDed with
// ConsecutiveBarsLittleOverlap. Those two measure the overlap as a FRACTION of
// a bar's range and are direction-agnostic; this is a DIRECTIONAL, TICK-sized
// test on one extreme only -- a bull bar may overlap the prior bar's body
// completely and still pass, provided its low did not drop more than a tick or
// two below the prior low. It is the reason the rule waited for the tick list.
// It is also not ThinAreaLowBarOverlap, which divides the shared span by the
// earlier bar's range and asks for a THIN area rather than a held low.
//
// V19-03's fixed two-bar first-hour variant is this rule at spikeBars = 2; its
// session gate is dropped, being TimeWindow in a separate pane. Brooks reads 2
// bars for a light always-in call and 3-4 for a trend day; 3 is the merged
// default.
//
// BOTH SIDES ARE COMPUTED IN THE HOOK, as flags cleared at the top: EasyLanguage
// does not short-circuit and has no loop break, so the loop must run to
// completion in both twins and the twins must fail the same way. The body test
// is a multiplication, never a ratio, so a zero-range bar cannot raise EL's
// divide-by-zero -- such a bar fails the strict body-direction test anyway.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage (README "N ticks",
// TICKSIZE_FINDINGS.md). Read once into a local; it needs no history.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
okUp = true;
okDn = true;
for (kk = 0; kk <= (int)spikeBars - 1; ++kk) {
    if (el_le(close[kk], open[kk]) || el_lt(close[kk] - open[kk], bodyFraction * (high[kk] - low[kk]))) okUp = false;
    if (el_ge(close[kk], open[kk]) || el_lt(open[kk] - close[kk], bodyFraction * (high[kk] - low[kk]))) okDn = false;
    if (kk < (int)spikeBars - 1) {
        if (el_lt(low[kk], low[kk + 1] - maxOverlapTicks * tickSz)) okUp = false;
        if (el_gt(high[kk], high[kk + 1] + maxOverlapTicks * tickSz)) okDn = false;
    }
}
